From 773ee7b244c5b4d0ad58e8e1180a006673ea2450 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christian Hewitt <christianshewitt@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 13:04:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 199/210] arm64: dts: amlogic: meson-s4: add CPU frequency
 scaling

The CPU clocks on S4 are programmed by the secure firmware. Reference
the secure monitor from the PLL clock controller so it can register
them, and add the CPU clock, a binned OPP table and a CPU cooling map so
cpufreq can scale the CPU frequency and the thermal framework can
throttle it.

The OPP table gives four sets of voltages, one per bin, and points at
the secure monitor that reports the bin. Bins 0 and 1 are identical,
and are the same for the S905Y4 and S805X2 up to 1.704GHz. Bins 2 and 3
run the same frequencies at up to 100 mV less. Add the faster OPPs in a
dtsi for each variant: 1.8GHz, 1.908GHz and 2.004GHz for the S905Y4
used by the Khadas VIM1S, and 1.8GHz for the S805X2 used by the AQ222.
The S905Y4 rates come from sys_pll_dco between 3.6GHz and 4.008GHz
divided by two.

The 666MHz OPP is fclk_div3, so use its exact rate.

1.404GHz is left out. It needs sys_pll_dco at 5616MHz divided by 4, and
although the PLL reports lock there, the measured CPU clock wanders
between 1.31GHz and 1.35GHz.

The CPU cores of the VIM1S and the AQ222 are supplied by the VDDCPU PWM
regulator, so reference it from the CPU nodes. The regulator output is
filtered and needs time to reach a new voltage, while cpufreq-dt
switches to a higher frequency as soon as the voltage is set, so the
CPU briefly runs the new frequency at the old voltage. Switching
between 100MHz and 2.004GHz under load corrupted memory within two
minutes on a VIM1S. Give the regulator a 150uV/us ramp delay, about
30mV per 200us, which avoids it.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Christian Hewitt <christianshewitt@gmail.com>
